::JOUR_U01_L01_Q01::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
A reporter needs to tell readers that the bus schedule changed starting Monday. Which format best fits this purpose? {
=News brief#Correct. A news brief is the best fit for a timely, factual announcement.
~Feature profile#Incorrect. A feature profile focuses on a person in depth, not a short schedule update.
~Opinion column#Incorrect. This situation requires facts, not a personal argument.
~Photo essay#Incorrect. A photo essay relies on a sequence of images, which is not needed here.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q02::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which statement best describes a journalism publication format? {
~A decoration choice that has no effect on meaning#Incorrect. Format affects structure, language, and audience expectations.
~A private notebook style used only by the writer#Incorrect. Journalism formats are created for a public audience.
=A type of journalism piece with accepted rules and purposes#Correct. Format guides how the journalist creates quality work.
~A way to avoid checking facts#Incorrect. Every journalism format still requires accuracy.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q03::MLA.JOUR.NEWS.06
Which wording is most appropriate for factual journalism? {
~The new club is obviously the best activity ever.#Incorrect. This is opinion language and is not supported by facts.
=The new club meets Tuesdays in Room 214 after school.#Correct. This gives precise, factual information.
~Everyone should join because it is amazing.#Incorrect. This persuades rather than reports.
~The club thing is happening somewhere soon.#Incorrect. This wording is too vague for journalism.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q04::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
A caption under a photograph of students planting trees should primarily do what? {
~Argue that tree planting is the only important school activity#Incorrect. That would turn the caption into an unsupported opinion.
~Describe every detail of the full event article#Incorrect. A caption should be concise and connected to the image.
~Avoid naming what appears in the photograph#Incorrect. Identification is a key caption rule.
=Identify the people or action shown and connect the image to accurate information#Correct. A quality caption makes the image clear and factual.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q05::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Why might a school publication use a short video instead of only a written article? {
~Video removes the need to be accurate.#Incorrect. Accuracy is still required in video journalism.
~Video means the audience no longer matters.#Incorrect. Format choice should always consider the audience.
=Video can show sound, movement, and visual details that fit the story purpose.#Correct. Media tools can change how information is communicated.
~Video is always easier than writing a fact-based article.#Incorrect. Each format has its own rules and challenges.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q06::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which sentence best follows the rule for a straight news brief? {
~The cafeteria change is exciting and proves the school is improving.#Incorrect. This adds unsupported opinion to a factual format.
~I think the cafeteria change will make everyone happier.#Incorrect. First-person opinion does not fit a straight news brief.
~Students will probably love the change because breakfast is great.#Incorrect. This predicts reactions without evidence.
=The cafeteria will begin serving breakfast at 7:15 a.m. on Tuesday.#Correct. This sentence reports a clear fact directly.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q07::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
What is the main problem when a writer uses the same structure for every journalism format? {
=The piece may not meet the audience's expectations for that format.#Correct. Different formats require different structures and rules.
~The piece will automatically become more accurate.#Incorrect. Using one structure for all formats does not improve accuracy.
~The piece will become too factual for readers.#Incorrect. Factuality is not the problem; mismatched format is.
~The piece will always become an opinion column.#Incorrect. It may become confusing, but not always an opinion column.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q08::MLA.JOUR.NEWS.06
Which word pair best fits journalism vocabulary in this lesson? {
~Imaginary and magical#Incorrect. These terms fit creative writing more than factual journalism formats.
=Audience and attribution#Correct. Both words are central to journalism purpose and format quality.
~Theme and symbolism#Incorrect. These are mainly literary analysis terms, not the focus of this lesson.
~Equation and variable#Incorrect. These terms do not fit journalism format work.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q09::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
An opinion column is different from a news brief because an opinion column should usually include what? {
~Only a photo caption#Incorrect. A caption is a different format.
~No clear viewpoint#Incorrect. Opinion columns need a clear viewpoint.
=A claim supported by reasons or evidence#Correct. Opinion writing should make and support a viewpoint.
~A list of facts with no position#Incorrect. That better describes straight news reporting.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q10::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which choice best explains format-specific quality work? {
~Making the piece longer than every other article#Incorrect. Length alone does not create quality.
~Using the writer's favorite style no matter the assignment#Incorrect. Quality depends on matching the format and purpose.
~Avoiding audience needs while focusing on decoration#Incorrect. Audience needs are central to journalism.
=Following the accepted rules of the chosen format to communicate clearly#Correct. Format rules guide quality journalism.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q11::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
A digital story includes text, a map, and a short audio clip. What does this show about journalism formats? {
~All journalism must now ignore writing rules.#Incorrect. Writing rules still matter in digital formats.
=Technology can combine media types to communicate information.#Correct. Digital tools can change how journalism is presented.
~A story with audio cannot be factual.#Incorrect. Audio journalism can be factual and accurate.
~Maps should replace all reporting.#Incorrect. Maps can support reporting but do not replace it.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q12::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which question should a journalist ask first when choosing a format? {
~How can I make this sound like my favorite novel?#Incorrect. Journalism format choice begins with factual purpose and audience.
=What does the audience need to know, and how should this information be presented?#Correct. Purpose and audience guide format choice.
~How can I include as many opinions as possible?#Incorrect. Opinion belongs only in appropriate formats and must be supported.
~Can I leave out the main facts?#Incorrect. Journalism depends on clear facts.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q13::MLA.JOUR.NEWS.06
Which sentence shows clear, audience-friendly academic vocabulary? {
=The update affects students who reserve the library study room.#Correct. The sentence uses precise words that fit the audience and topic.
~The stuff changes for whoever uses that place.#Incorrect. This wording is vague and unhelpful.
~The amazing thing is doing an update thing.#Incorrect. The wording is unclear and informal.
~Everybody will totally understand what I mean.#Incorrect. This does not provide clear information.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q14::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which format would best allow more background, scene detail, and human interest about a volunteer project? {
~News brief#Incorrect. A news brief is shorter and more direct.
~Photo caption#Incorrect. A caption identifies an image but does not usually develop background.
=Feature story#Correct. A feature can develop context and human detail.
~Correction notice#Incorrect. A correction notice fixes an error; it is not the best format here.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q15::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
What makes a sentence inappropriate for a factual news brief? {
=It includes unsupported personal judgment.#Correct. Unsupported judgment does not fit straight factual reporting.
~It gives the date of an event.#Incorrect. Dates are useful facts in a news brief.
~It names the location of an event.#Incorrect. Location helps the reader understand the news.
~It identifies who is involved.#Incorrect. Identifying people or groups is often necessary.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q16::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which example best shows a change in journalism caused by technology? {
~A writer checks spelling before publication.#Incorrect. Editing is important, but it is not mainly a technology shift in format.
~A reporter interviews a source respectfully.#Incorrect. Respectful interviewing is a journalism skill, not a media technology change.
~A class reads a printed newspaper silently.#Incorrect. This uses a traditional format, not a change caused by newer media tools.
=A publication adds interactive charts to an online article.#Correct. Interactive tools show how technology can change media presentation.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q17::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which rule should guide a multimedia journalism post? {
~Use media even when it distracts from the information.#Incorrect. Media should support the purpose, not distract from it.
=Choose text, image, audio, or video elements that help the audience understand the facts.#Correct. Multimedia choices should serve the journalism purpose.
~Avoid all facts because multimedia is visual.#Incorrect. Multimedia journalism still requires accurate facts.
~Use only opinion because digital readers expect it.#Incorrect. Digital formats can be factual, persuasive, or explanatory depending on purpose.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q18::MLA.JOUR.NEWS.06
Which revision improves journalistic precision? {
~Change "students" to "some people or whatever."#Incorrect. This makes the wording less precise.
=Change "soon" to "Monday at 8:00 a.m."#Correct. A specific time is clearer and more useful for readers.
~Change "reported" to "felt like saying."#Incorrect. That weakens journalistic tone and precision.
~Change "library" to "place."#Incorrect. This removes useful detail.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q19::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which statement is true about format rules? {
~They matter only in professional newspapers, not school journalism.#Incorrect. Format rules also matter in school publications.
~They prevent writers from using any facts.#Incorrect. Format rules help organize facts effectively.
=They help readers understand what kind of journalism they are reading.#Correct. Format rules signal purpose and structure to the audience.
~They make audience awareness unnecessary.#Incorrect. Format rules are closely connected to audience needs.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q20::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
A writer adds "in my opinion" throughout a piece meant to report a school safety update. What is the main issue? {
~The piece has too many dates.#Incorrect. The issue is opinion language, not dates.
~The piece names a public location.#Incorrect. Naming a location can be useful in factual reporting.
=The piece does not match the factual purpose of the format.#Correct. A safety update should report clear facts, not personal opinion.
~The piece has no headline.#Incorrect. A headline may be needed, but the larger issue is the mismatch in purpose.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q21::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
What should stay constant even when journalism moves from print to digital formats? {
~The exact page size#Incorrect. Digital formats may not use the same page size as print.
~The absence of visuals#Incorrect. Digital journalism often uses visuals.
~The refusal to consider audience#Incorrect. Audience awareness remains important.
=The need for accuracy and clear purpose#Correct. Format may change, but quality standards remain.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q22::MLA.JOUR.NEWS.06
Which phrase is clearest for a journalism audience? {
=The robotics team won the regional competition Saturday.#Correct. This phrase gives specific, factual information.
~The team did a thing sometime.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~The best people totally crushed it.#Incorrect. This is informal opinion, not clear reporting.
~Stuff happened with robots.#Incorrect. This does not communicate useful information.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q23::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which format best fits a piece that argues students should recycle more and gives reasons? {
~News brief#Incorrect. A news brief reports facts without arguing a position.
=Opinion column#Correct. An opinion column presents a viewpoint and supports it.
~Photo caption#Incorrect. A caption identifies an image and provides brief context.
~Event listing#Incorrect. An event listing announces details, not an argument.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q24::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which response best explains why a format rule matters? {
~It lets the writer ignore facts if the piece sounds good.#Incorrect. Journalism format rules do not remove the need for facts.
~It makes every journalism piece sound exactly the same.#Incorrect. Different formats have different rules.
=It helps the writer meet the reader's expectations for that type of piece.#Correct. Format rules support clarity, trust, and audience understanding.
~It matters only after publication is finished.#Incorrect. Format rules guide the writing process before publication.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_Q25::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which checklist best fits Lesson 1 mastery? {
~Choose any format, add opinion, and skip the audience.#Incorrect. This ignores the lesson's focus on purpose, audience, and accepted rules.
~Write creatively, hide sources, and use dramatic language.#Incorrect. Journalism requires accuracy and clear format choices.
~Use the longest format possible and avoid precise vocabulary.#Incorrect. Length does not determine quality, and precise vocabulary matters.
=Identify the format, explain its purpose, and follow its accepted rules.#Correct. This matches format-specific quality work.
}
